45417] [17592186152297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186486423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152297 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152297 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152297 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "88d0f4ad-a3f9-5275-b5d5-a9973bf63ebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152298 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152298 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152298 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152298 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186455511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152298 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Word Problems Involving Percentage Increases Greater Than 100%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152298 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152298 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bbb0103d-d686-575a-8ef8-81640227f4dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1045 "Applying Percentage Increases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152315 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b42cbc99-dc3a-5ea5-af15-2e43d6dacdce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153298 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153298 #edb/ref 1061 615692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "547ba33b-b6b3-5bae-b2dc-eacefe1e81a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153299 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153299 #edb/ref 1061 615693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153299 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a946c7fa-5aee-54d2-b223-0a3b560b3401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153299 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153300 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153300 #edb/ref 1061 615696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153300 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd57418f-a99a-5c17-9192-9191eca0084b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153300 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153301 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153301 #edb/ref 1061 615700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153301 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3bd6e9e-9d93-51a6-9a20-5e9292cf93f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153301 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153302 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153302 #edb/ref 1061 615701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153302 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9b4f520-6201-594a-907d-e7a899c8d57c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153302 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153303 #edb/ref 1061 615704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153303 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10eca415-b601-5bc8-80c2-c2db5a9c451e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153303 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153325 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes we need to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a so-called **percentage increase.**\n\nTo apply a percentage increase, we add the increase to $100\\%,$ convert the result to a decimal multiplier, and then find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following problem.\n\nWhat number is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160?$\n\nThe key words here are \"$\\boldsymbol{25\\%}$ **greater than.**\" This means we need to apply a percentage increase, increasing the original number by $25\\%.$\n\nTo find a number $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 25\\% = 125\\%.\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $125\\%$ of $160.$ \n\nSo, first, we convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125\\% = \\dfrac{125}{100} = 1.25\n$$\n\nTo find a number that is $25\\%$ greater than $160,$ we multiply $160$ by $1.25{:}$\n\n$$\n1.25 \\cdot 160 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $25\\%$ *greater than* $160$ is $200.$\n\nThis method applies to *any* percentage increase, including those greater than $100\\%{;}$ add the increase to $100\\%$ before converting to a decimal multiplier." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153325 #edb/ref 1065 14336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153325 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "83dbd64f-3ab9-5239-b54b-ae6d91816248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153325 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153340 #edb/ref 1064 "It's possible to increase a number by $100\\%$ or more. \n\nFor example, what number is $150\\%$ greater than $90?$\n\nTo find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $90,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90,$ we multiply $90$ by $2.5:$\n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 90 = 225\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $150\\%$ greater than $90$ is $225.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153340 #edb/ref 